  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nLow pressure system that has brought lots of rain to the Atlantic \ncoast is continuing to work its way up the eastern coastline of the \nUnited States. Currently we're still getting a couple of lighter \nbands of precipitation on the western edge of this system in the \nsouthern Appalachians. Rain has been slow to work it's way through \nthe region, but it is tapering off early this afternoon and this \ntrend should continue through the rest of the day. Low clouds will \nstick around however. A brief clearing of the clouds could occur as \nMiddle TN remains mostly sunny as of this writing. However any \nglances at the sky will not last long as our next system approaches \nthe southern Appalachians. \n\nA shortwave is moving towards us from the west and will usher in a \ncold front and quick shot of rain early Monday morning. Moisture \nwill be quite limited with this system, but there will be some \nplaces that get a quick sprinkle, maybe up to a tenth of an inch of \nrain as the front moves through. Behind the front on Monday \nafternoon, a NW flow develops that will keep some rain chances in \nthe East TN mountains until the evening. Cooler temperatures follow \nthe front on Tuesday, with highs in the 50s. \n\nBy the mid-week, a trough is expected to swing across the Deep \nSouth, bringing a return of rain chances. This system looks to bring \nbetter chances for widespread rain, and higher rainfall amounts. \nDepending on the amplification of this system and track of the \nsurface low , we could potentially see some breezy winds across the \nmountains and foothills. Current timing for rain chances with this \nsystem appear to be Wednesday and Wednesday night, perhaps lingering \ninto Thanksgiving Day. Dry conditions and near normal temperatures \ncan be expected behind this system for the end of the week.",
